  "record_id": "019",
  "problem": "Werner–Holevo zero-capacity boundary",
  "collection": "Original research investigation",
  "summary": "Swap invariance was replaced by the strictly stronger requirement of support in the symmetric subspace.",
  "review_status": "source_reviewed",
  "review_note": "No open interpretation blocker was recorded in the preceding source review. This is not a new independent mathematical verification.",
  "original": {
    "excerpt": "class ExtensionSDP:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.rho = cp.Variable((SYM_DIM, SYM_DIM), symmetric=True, name=\"rho\")\n        self.t = cp.Variable(name=\"t\")\n        self.x = cp.Parameter(name=\"x\")\n        self.eta = V @ self.rho @ V.T",
    "context": "V is the isometry onto A tensor Sym^2(B), of dimension 3 times 6 = 18. The frozen task asked for an extension invariant under swapping two qutrit B systems, not necessarily supported only in their symmetric subspace.",
    "locator": "Semidefinite-program script creation, 2026-07-28T22:15:31.150Z, JSONL line 130"
  },
  "witness": {
    "explanation": "Swap invariance was replaced by the strictly stronger requirement of support in the symmetric subspace.",
    "evidence": "Let v=(|01>-|10>)/sqrt(2) and X=|0><0|_A tensor |v><v|. X is positive semidefinite and obeys S X S=X because Sv=-v. It is therefore swap-invariant, but its support lies entirely in the antisymmetric B sector and cannot be written V rho V^T. In the qutrit case the omitted block has dimension 3 times 3 = 9. The code solves a restricted feasibility problem, so its infeasibility cannot certify the original criterion.",
    "scope": "argument_invalid"
  },
